# nl-startup-2: hung-core detection in the desktop (apply in apps/desktop/src/main/protocol/local-core.ts and the host that calls resolveLocalCore)
1. local-core.ts, function readCoreState: add to the returned object (and to its return type)
     controlPort: Number.isInteger(discovery.core?.control?.port) ? discovery.core.control.port : null,
     token: typeof discovery.endpoints?.session?.token === 'string' ? discovery.endpoints.session.token : null,
   (the parsed type gains core.control{port} and endpoints.session{token}), and export readCoreState (step 2 calls it
   from the module that holds the launched child).
2. main/index.ts (hot file): in createProtocol's local branch, inside the resolveLocalCore onLaunched callback that sets
   localCoreProcess, start the watcher once per app run (keep it in a module-level `let coreWatcher`, started only when null):
     coreWatcher ??= watchCore({
       readDiscovery: () => readCoreState(resolveRuntimeRoot()),
       ownedPid: () => localCoreProcess?.pid ?? null,   // the child onLaunched last reported; a pid that no longer runs is skipped by watchCore
       log: (line) => logger.error(line),
     });
   import { watchCore } from '../../../../../packages/protocol-client/src/launch/ensure-core.mjs'; call coreWatcher?.stop() at quit
   (before the coreExit(localCoreProcess, ...) wait). No onKilled is needed: the connection's reconnect ladder calls resolveLocalCore, whose ensureCore
   starts a replacement because the killed pid names no core.
